te10†GitHub Advanced SecurityEnterprise-grade security features L101:       * cite57†Copilot for BusinessEnterprise-grade AI features L102:       * cite58†Premium SupportEnterprise-grade 24/7 support L103: \nL104:   * cite59†Pricing L105: \nL106: Search`/`\nL107: \nL108: cite1†Sign in L109: \nL110: cite60†Sign up L111: \nL112: Appearance settings\nL113: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL114: \nL115: cite61†zeux / cite62†meshoptimizer Public\nL116: \nL117:   * cite63†Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\nL118:   * cite63†Fork 752 L119:   * cite63†Star L120:   * cite62†Code L121:   * cite64†Issues 4 L122:   * cite65†Pull requests 2 L123:   * cite66†Discussions L124:   * cite67†Actions L125:   * cite68†Security and quality 0 L126:   * cite69†Insights L127: \nL128: Additional navigation options\nL129: \nL130: master\nL131: \nL132: cite70†Branches cite71†Tags L133: \nL134: [Input: Go to file]\nL135: \nL136: Go to file\nL137: \nL138: Code\nL139: \nL140: Open more actions menu\nL141: ## Latest commit\nL142: \nL143: \nL144: \nL145: ## History\nL146: \nL147: 4,244 Commits\nL148: ## Folders and files\nL149: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL150: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL151: cite72†.github | cite72†.github |    |\nL152: cite73†demo | cite73†demo |    |\nL153: cite74†extern | cite74†extern |    |\nL154: cite75†gltf | cite75†gltf |    |\nL155: cite76†js | cite76†js |    |\nL156: cite77†src | cite77†src |    |\nL157: cite78†tools | cite78†tools |    |\nL158: cite79†.clang-format | cite79†.clang-format |    |\nL159: cite80†.editorconfig | cite80†.editorconfig |    |\nL160: cite81†.git-blame-ignore-revs | cite81†.git-blame-ignore-revs |    |\nL161: cite82†.gitignore | cite82†.gitignore |    |\nL162: cite83†.prettierrc | cite83†.prettierrc |    |\nL163: cite84†CMakeLists.txt | cite84†CMakeLists.txt |    |\nL164: cite85†CONTRIBUTING.md | cite85†CONTRIBUTING.md |    |\nL165: cite86†LICENSE.md | cite86†LICENSE.md |    |\nL166: cite87†Makefile | cite87†Makefile |    |\nL167: cite88†README.md | cite88†README.md |    |\nL168: [Button: View all files]\nL169: ## Repository files navigation\nL170: \nL171:   *   * cite89†README L172:   * cite89†Contributing L173:   * cite89†MIT license L174: \nL175: More items\nL176: \nL177: # 🐇 meshoptimizer\nL178: ## Purpose\nL179: \nL180: When a GPU renders triangle meshes, various stages of the GPU pipeline have to process vertex and index data. The efficiency of these stages depends on the data you feed to them; this library provides algorithms to help optimize meshes for these stages, as well as algorithms to reduce the mesh complexity and storage overhead.\nL181: The library provides a C and C++ interface for all algorithms; you can use it from C/C++ or from other languages via FFI (such as P/Invoke). If you want to use this library from Rust, you should use cite90†meshopt crate†crates.io . JavaScript interface for some algorithms is available through cite91†meshoptimizer.js†www.npmjs.com .\nL182: Two companion projects are developed and distributed alongside the library: cite92†gltfpack , a command-line tool that automatically optimizes glTF files, and cite93†clusterlod.h , a single-header C/C++ library for continuous level of detail using clustered simplification.\nL183: ## Installing\nL184: \nL185: meshoptimizer is hosted on GitHub; you can download the latest release using git:\nL186: \nL187:     `git clone -b v1.3 https://github.com/zeux/meshoptimizer.git\nL188:     `\nL189: \nL190: Alternatively you can cite94†download the .zip archive from GitHub .\nL191: The library is also available as a Linux package in several distributions (cite95†ArchLinux†aur.archlinux.org , cite96†Debian†packages.debian.org , cite97†FreeBSD†www.freshports.org , cite98†Nix†mynixos.com , cite99†Ubuntu†packages.ubuntu.com ), as well as a cite100†Vcpkg port (see cite101†installation instructions†learn.microsoft.com ) and a cite102†Conan package†conan.io .\nL192: cite92†gltfpack is available as a pre-built binary on cite103†Releases page or via cite104†npm package†www.npmjs.com . Native binaries are recommended since they are more efficient and support texture compression.\nL193: ## Building\nL194: \nL195: meshoptimizer is distributed as a C/C++ header (`src/meshoptimizer.h`) and a set of C++ source files (`src/*.cpp`). To include it in your project, you can use one of two options:\nL196: \nL197:   * Use CMake to build the library (either as a standalone project or as part of your project)\nL198:   * Add source files to your project's build system\nL199: The source files are organized in such a way that you don't need to change your build-system settings, and you only need to add the source files for the algorithms you use. They should build without warnings or special compilation options on all major compilers. If you prefer amalgamated builds, you can also concatenate the source files into a single `.cpp` file and build that instead.\nL200: To use meshoptimizer functions, simply `#include` the header `meshoptimizer.h`; the library source is C++, but the header is C-compatible.\nL201: ## Core pipeline\nL202: \nL203: When optimizing a mesh, to maximize rendering efficiency you should typically feed it through a set of optimizations (the order is important!):\nL204: \nL205:   1. Indexing\nL206:   2. Vertex cache optimization\nL207:   3. (optional) Overdraw optimization\nL208:   4. Vertex fetch optimization\nL209:   5. Vertex quantization\nL210:   6. Index filtering\nL211:   7. (optional) Shadow indexing\nL212: ### Indexing\nL213: \nL214: Most algorithms in this library assume that a mesh has a vertex buffer and an index buffer. For algorithms to work well and also for GPU to render your mesh efficiently, the vertex buffer has to have no redundant vertices; you can generate an index buffer from an unindexed vertex buffer or reindex an existing (potentially redundant) index buffer as follows:\nL215: > Note: meshoptimizer generally works with 32-bit (`unsigned int`) indices, however when using C++ APIs you can use any integer type for index data by using the provided template overloads. By convention, remap tables always use `unsigned int`.\nL216: First, generate a remap table from your existing vertex (and, optionally, index) data:\nL217: \nL218:     size_t index_count = face_count * 3;\nL219:     size_t unindexed_vertex_count = face_count * 3;\nL220:     std::vector<unsigned int> remap(unindexed_vertex_count); // temporary remap table\nL221:     size_t vertex_count = meshopt_generateVertexRemap(&remap[0], NULL, index_count,\nL222:         &unindexed_vertices[0], unindexed_vertex_count, sizeof(Vertex));\nL223: Note that in this case we only have an unindexed vertex buffer; when input mesh has an index buffer, it will need to be passed to `meshopt_generateVertexRemap` instead of `NULL`, along with the correct source vertex count. In either case, the remap table is generated based on binary equivalence of the input vertices, so the resulting mesh will render the same way. Binary equivalence considers all input bytes, including padding which should be zero-initialized if the vertex structure has gaps.\nL224: After generating the remap table, you can allocate space for the target vertex buffer (`vertex_count` elements) and index buffer (`index_count` elements) and generate them:\nL225: \nL226:     meshopt_remapIndexBuffer(indices, NULL, index_count, &remap[0]);\nL227:     meshopt_remapVertexBuffer(vertices, &unindexed_vertices[0], unindexed_vertex_count, sizeof(Vertex), &remap[0]);\nL228: \nL229: You can then further optimize the resulting buffers by calling the other functions on them in-place.\nL230: `meshopt_generateVertexRemap` uses binary equivalence of vertex data, which is generally a reasonable default; however, in some cases some attributes may have floating point drift causing extra vertices to be generated. For such cases, it may be necessary to quantize some attributes (most importantly, normals and tangents) before generating the remap, or use `meshopt_generateVertexRemapCustom` algorithm that allows comparing individual attributes with tolerance by providing a custom comparison function:\nL231: \nL232:     size_t vertex_count = meshopt_generateVertexRemapCustom(&remap[0], NULL, index_count,\nL233:         &unindexed_vertices[0].px, unindexed_vertex_count, sizeof(Vertex),\nL234:         [&](unsigned int lhs, unsigned int rhs) -> bool {\nL235:             const Vertex& lv = unindexed_vertices[lhs];\nL236:             const Vertex& rv = unindexed_vertices[rhs];\nL237: \nL238:             return fabsf(lv.tx - rv.tx) < 1e-3f && fabsf(lv.ty - rv.ty) < 1e-3f;\nL239:         });\nL240: ### Vertex cache optimization\nL241: When the GPU renders the mesh, it runs the vertex shader for each vertex. Historically, GPUs used a small fixed-size post-transform cache (16-32 vertices) with different replacement policies to store the shader output and avoid redundant shader invocations.\nL242: Modern GPUs still perform vertex reuse, but with substantially different mechanics: vertex invocations are batched into thread groups based on the input indices, and effective reuse depends on factors like vertex shader outputs and rasterizer throughput. To maximize the locality of reused vertex references, you have to reorder your triangles like so:\nL243: \nL244:     meshopt_optimizeVertexCache(indices, indices, index_count, vertex_count);\nL245: The details of vertex reuse vary between different GPU architectures, so vertex cache optimization uses an adaptive algorithm that produces a triangle sequence with good locality that works well across different GPUs. Alternatively, you can use an algorithm that optimizes specifically for fixed-size FIFO caches: `meshopt_optimizeVertexCacheFifo` (with a recommended cache size of 16).\nL246: While it generally produces less performant results on most GPUs, it runs ~2x faster, which may benefit rapid content iteration.\nL247: ### Overdraw optimization\nL248: After transforming the vertices, GPU sends the triangles for rasterization which results in generating pixels that are usually first run through the depth test, and pixels that pass it get the pixel shader executed to generate the final color. As pixel shaders get more expensive, it becomes more and more important to reduce overdraw.\nL249: While in general improving overdraw requires view-dependent operations, this library provides an algorithm to reorder triangles to minimize the overdraw from all directions, which you can run after vertex cache optimization like this:\nL250: \nL251:     meshopt_optimizeOverdraw(indices, indices, index_count, &vertices[0].x, vertex_count, sizeof(Vertex), 1.05f);\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a101e4-6089-7ec1-acf2-e00d6778da4f","create_time":1791033774.513414}}}
