{"timestamp":"2026-10-03T13:40:10.522Z","ordinal":18004,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a101fe-6099-74a3-b794-fa21dba4458f","author":"/root/gpu_shader_review","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/gpu_shader_review\nPayload:\n**One worthwhile next shader experiment: pack ground roughness into the diffuse texture’s alpha channel.**\n\nExact location: [TownGround.swift:142](/Users/thomi/Projects/entireio-marvin/apps/simulator-macos/Sources/MarvinSimulator/TownGround.swift:142) through the surface modifier at line 156.\n\nCurrently the town ground samples three separate 2048×2048 maps—base color, normal and roughness—with identical UVs, repeat wrapping, linear mip filtering and anisotropy 8. Keep the base RGB and normal map unchanged, place the existing roughness values in base alpha, remove the separate roughness texture, then begin the surface modifier with:\n\n```metal\n_surface.roughness = _surface.diffuse.a;\n_surface.diffuse.a = 1.0;\n```\n\nKeep the existing pigment, lighting, normal intensity and fragment-stage analytic blend unchanged. Use an explicit unpremultiplied Metal texture; an ordinary AppKit RGBA image path could premultiply RGB by the new alpha and corrupt the appearance.\n\n**Why this candidate:** Xcode’s existing M2 capture attributes 8.692% shader execution cost to town-ground main shading, with 1,231,552 fragment invocations and 23.24 sampler calls per fragment. Packing can eliminate one anisotropically filtered material lookup without dropping texture resolution or material detail. I found no previous roughness-channel-packing experiment in the source, renderer/profiling documents or saved experiment text. This differs from the rejected procedural-noise lookup.\n\n**Narrow experiment:**\n\n1. Inspect Xcode’s original diffuse/roughness resource formats and mip chains. Preserve their decoded values and filtering semantics.\n2. First validate a packing control: identical RGB, alpha one, original separate roughness. This must reproduce the original image before interpreting a candidate.\n3. Validate sampled roughness separately, including mip levels and grazing angles; then enable the packed-alpha roughness path.\n4. Run fresh-process A/B/B/A comparisons with fixed verified state at 1080p/2× MSAA. Compare clear, low-sun and storm ground close-ups, silhouettes, tracks and both blend boundaries. Avoid the known shader-toggle restoration problem.\n5. Capture the candidate in Xcode: verify the separate roughness resource/sample disappeared and compare main-pass sampler calls, shader time and whole-frame GPU time. Only advance if the saving exceeds bracket drift and image review passes.\n\n**Limitations:** SceneKit could preprocess alpha or material roughness; verify generated code and pixels rather than assuming the two assignments suffice. JPEG decode/color conversion, sRGB handling and mip generation can introduce differences. Apple documents that sRGB texture components convert to linear before filtering, so the packed asset must preserve that behavior. [Apple Metal texture guide](https://developer-mdn.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/Miscellaneous/Conceptual/MetalProgrammingGuide/Mem-Obj/Mem-Obj.html)\n\nNo predictable millisecond gain yet; AO preparation may already eliminate unused roughness work. Dust’s high cost remains, but its already rejected zero-alpha/vertex-tint changes provide less promising next work. No files changed or rendering workloads started."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a101fa-8585-7632-b697-28e4fd61ef3f","create_time":1791034810.521957}}}
