t\n\n# CAD sources and interchange formats\n*.3dm   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.step  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.stp   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.f3d   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.iges  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n\n# Media and datasheets\n*.pdf   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.png   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.jpg   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.mp4   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# The published website is the exception to all of the above\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# GitHub Pages serves files straight out of the repository and does NOT resolve\n# Git LFS pointers. An LFS-tracked image or video reaches the browser as a\n# ~130-byte text file, so the page silently breaks. These undo the media rules\n# above for the published site only.\n#\n# Scope this to the patterns that actually match an LFS rule. A blanket\n# `docs/** -filter -diff -merge` also unsets `diff`, which makes Git treat the\n# site's HTML, CSS and JS as binary and stop showing their changes.\napps/build-docs/**/*.png  -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/build-docs/**/*.jpg  -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/build-docs/**/*.webp -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/build-docs/**/*.mp4  -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/build-docs/**/*.mov  -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/build-docs/**/*.pdf  -filter -diff -merge -text\n\n# Application and test screenshots ship directly from the repository too.\napps/web/**/*.png          -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/web/**/*.jpg          -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/web/**/*.mp4          -filter -diff -merge -text\ntests/acceptance/**/*.png  -filter -diff -merge -text\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Line endings\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n* text=auto eol=lf\n*.sh    text eol=lf\n*.bat   text eol=crlf\n\n# KiCad files are text and should diff as text\n*.kicad_sch  text\n*.kicad_pcb  text\n*.kicad_pro  text\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# GitHub language statistics\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nhardware/mechanical/* linguist-documentation\napps/build-docs/*     linguist-documentation\n\n# Offline human art source and generated crowd geometry.\nscripts/city/*.blend filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\napps/simulator-macos/Resources/City/crowd.json filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\napps/simulator-macos/Resources/City/*.hdr filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":3125},"formatted_output":"# Marvin — Mechanical\n\nCAD sources and printable meshes for Marvin's chassis, tracks, and head.\n\n```\nhardware/mechanical/\n├── src/        CAD models (Rhino 3D, .3dm)\n├── step/       Whole-robot STEP, for other CAD packages\n├── stl/        Meshes exported for printing\n└── README.md   You are here\n```\n\n---\n\n## Parts\n\nParts are numbered in print order: the PLA parts first — chassis, track\ncovers, wheels, then the head — and the one TPU part last.\n\n| # | Part | File | Qty | Material |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 01 | Body / chassis | `01_body.stl` | 1 | PLA |\n| 02 | Track cover, left | `02_track_cover_left.stl` | 1 | PLA |\n| 03 | Track cover, right | `03_track_cover_right.stl` | 1 | PLA |\n| 04 | Wheel | `04_wheel.stl` | 4 | PLA |\n| 05 | Head base | `05_head_base.stl` | 1 | PLA |\n| 06 | Head cover | `06_head_cover.stl` | 1 | PLA |\n| 07 | Neck | `07_neck.stl` | 1 | PLA |\n| 08 | Neck mount | `08_neck_mount.stl` | 1 | PLA |\n| 09 | Track link | `09_track.stl` | many | TPU |\n\nThe eight PLA parts are 01–08; the track link is the only part in TPU, so it\nsits last.\n\nThe numeric prefix is the only thing tying a mesh to its place in the assembly,\nso keep the sequence contiguous and unique. If you add a part, renumber rather\nthan reusing or skipping a number, and update this table in the same commit.\n\nWheel and track-link quantities are not yet confirmed against the assembly, and\nthere is no printed-parts BOM with fasteners, bearings, and motor\nspecifications yet. Both are open items.\n\n---\n\n## Print settings\n\nNot yet characterised — these are starting points, not validated settings.\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Material | PLA for structure; TPU for the track links |\n| Layer height | 0.08 mm |\n| Walls | 3 |\n| Infill | 20 % (structural parts), 40 % (neck and head mount) |\n| Supports | Tree on the body (01) and neck (07); normal on the wheels (04) |\n\nThe track links are the one part that genuinely needs a flexible filament —\nprinting them rigid gives you a robot that cannot corner. If you print a set,\nplease open an issue with your material, printer, and what worked.\n\n---\n\n## Whole-robot models\n\nTwo files carry the complete assembly — chassis, head, tracks, motors, servos,\nelectronics — rather than a single printable part:\n\n| File | Size | For |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `src/marvin_design_v3_hq.3dm` | 74 MB | Rhino, native |\n| `step/marvin_robot.step` | 28 MB | any CAD that reads STEP AP242 |\n\nBoth come from the 930 MB Rhino working model. Most of that bulk was not real\nNURBS: the heavy objects are degree-(1,1) 2x2 patches, i.e. flat triangles\nwearing a NURBS costume, inherited from the cinematic mesh the design started\nfrom. Converted to actual meshes they are ~100x cheaper and geometrically\nidentical — `02_track_cover_left` alone is 147 MB as a Brep and 1.3 MB as a\nmesh. Genuinely modelled parts (`07_neck`, `08_neck_mount`, bearings, battery,\nand 814 smaller objects) are still NURBS and still editable.\n\nAccuracy against the source is 0.0000 mm on the converted parts and never worse\nthan 0.05 mm anywhere in the STEP.\n\n**A caveat on the STEP.** It uses AP242 *tessellated* geometry, which is what\nmakes 700k triangles fit in 28 MB — as a B-rep the same mesh would be about\n700 MB, because a STEP face costs ~1 kB whether it holds a NURBS patch or a flat\ntriangle. Verified to open in OpenCASCADE and FreeCAD. **Rhino 8 does not read\ntessellated STEP** — Rhino users should take the `.3dm`, which is the same\ngeometry.\n\n## Large files\n\nOne file exceeds what GitHub will accept and is **not in this repository**:\n\n| File | Size | Why it is excluded |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `src/marvin_design.3dm` | 2.7 GB | Over GitHub's 100 MB file limit, and over Git LFS's 2 GB per-file cap |\n\nIt is listed in [`.gitignore`](../../.gitignore). To get it, ask for a copy — or,\nbetter, help fix the underlying problem.\n\n**The problem was tessellation, not complexity.** `01_body.stl` used to be a\n454 MB export — roughly nine million triangles for a part that is essentially a\nprinted box — and had to be distributed out of band. Re-exported from Rhino at\na coarser mesh tolerance it is 240 k triangles and 12 MB, with no visible\ndetail lost at print resolution, so it now lives in the repository like every\nother part. The whole `stl/` set is 22 MB.\n\nEvery mesh in `stl/`, the `.3dm` models and the `.step` export are tracked\nthrough Git LFS. Install LFS before cloning or you will get pointer files\ninstead of geometry:\n\n```bash\ngit lfs install\ngit clone https://github.com/spedemon/marvin.git\n```\n\nAlready cloned? `git lfs install && git lfs pull`.\n\n---\n\n## Conventions\n\n- **`src/` is the master; `stl/` is derived.** Never edit a mesh directly — make\n  the change in CAD and re-export, or the two drift apart irrecoverably.\n- Export in **binary** STL, never ASCII: same geometry, roughly a fifth the size.\n- Check the size of a mesh before committing it. Anything approaching 50 MB is\n  over-tessellated; re-export rather than commit.\n- **Rhino's ordinary Save re-adds the render-mesh cache** — it took the 74 MB\n  model back to 142 MB in one open-and-save. Use `SaveSmall` when committing.\n- Keep the numeric prefixes and update the table above when parts are added,\n  renamed, or renumbered.\n- Servo travel limits in the current\n  [`firmware/`](../../firmware/README.md) protect the neck from hitting its\n  mechanical stops. If the head geometry changes, update and physically verify\n  those limits in the same pull request.\n\n---\n\n## Licence\n\nEverything in this folder is licensed under the\n[CERN Open Hardware Licence v2, Strongly Reciprocal](../../LICENSE-hardware) —\nnot the MIT licence that covers the project's software.\nhardware/mechanical/src/marvin_design_v3_hq.3dm\nhardware/mechanical/README.md\nhardware/mechanical/stl/09_track.stl\nhardware/mechanical/stl/06_head_cover.stl\nhardware/mechanical/stl/04_wheel.stl\nhardware/mechanical/stl/03_track_cover_right.stl\nhardware/mechanical/stl/08_neck_mount.stl\nhardware/mechanical/stl/02_track_cover_left.stl\nhardware/mechanical/stl/01_body.stl\nhardware/mechanical/stl/05_head_base.stl\nhardware/mechanical/stl/07_neck.stl\nhardware/mechanical/step/marvin_robot.step\nhardware/mechanical/3d_print/marvin_BambuLab_x1C.3mf\nhardware/mechanical/3d_print/marvin_BambuLab_A1_Mini.3mf\nhardware/mechanical/3d_print/marvin_generic_printer.3mf\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Git LFS — large binary design assets\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# CAD and mesh files are binary and get re-exported often. Storing them in LFS\n# keeps them out of the packfile history, so a clone stays fast even after\n# dozens of design revisions.\n#\n# Contributors need Git LFS installed *before* cloning:\n#     git lfs install\n# If you cloned first, run `git lfs pull` to fetch the real files.\n\n# Meshes\n*.stl   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.3mf   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.obj   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.glb   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n\n# CAD sources and interchange formats\n*.3dm   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.step  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.stp   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.f3d   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.iges  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n\n# Media and datasheets\n*.pdf   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.png   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.jpg   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n*.mp4   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# The published website is the exception to all of the above\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# GitHub Pages serves files straight out of the repository and does NOT resolve\n# Git LFS pointers. An LFS-tracked image or video reaches the browser as a\n# ~130-byte text file, so the page silently breaks. These undo the media rules\n# above for the published site only.\n#\n# Scope this to the patterns that actually match an LFS rule. A blanket\n# `docs/** -filter -diff -merge` also unsets `diff`, which makes Git treat the\n# site's HTML, CSS and JS as binary and stop showing their changes.\napps/build-docs/**/*.png  -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/build-docs/**/*.jpg  -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/build-docs/**/*.webp -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/build-docs/**/*.mp4  -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/build-docs/**/*.mov  -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/build-docs/**/*.pdf  -filter -diff -merge -text\n\n# Application and test screenshots ship directly from the repository too.\napps/web/**/*.png          -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/web/**/*.jpg          -filter -diff -merge -text\napps/web/**/*.mp4          -filter -diff -merge -text\ntests/acceptance/**/*.png  -filter -diff -merge -text\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Line endings\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n* text=auto eol=lf\n*.sh    text eol=lf\n*.bat   text eol=crlf\n\n# KiCad files are text and should diff as text\n*.kicad_sch  text\n*.kicad_pcb  text\n*.kicad_pro  text\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# GitHub language statistics\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nhardware/mechanical/* linguist-documentation\napps/build-docs/*     linguist-documentation\n\n# Offline human art source and generated crowd geometry.\nscripts/city/*.blend filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\napps/simulator-macos/Resources/City/crowd.json filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\napps/simulator-macos/Resources/City/*.hdr filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\n"},"started_at_ms":1791034883615,"completed_at_ms":1791034883615}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-03T13:41:23.618Z","ordinal":18022,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a101ff-7e22-7032-b83e-53f77b510df3","call_id":"call_Le2nrb8oVmqyH9WESYZUu9Te","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"75e4ed\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000003125,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":2436,\"output\":\"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 2436)\\nTotal output lines: 235\\n\\n# Marvin — Mechanical\\n\\nCAD sources and printable meshes for Marvin's chassis, tracks, and head.\\n\\n```\\nhardware/mechanical/\\n├── src/        CAD models (Rhino 3D, .3dm)\\n├── step/       Whole-robot STEP, for other CAD packages\\n├── stl/        Meshes exported for printing\\n└── README.md   You are here\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Parts\\n\\nParts are numbered in print order: the PLA parts first — chassis, track\\ncovers, wheels, then the head — and the one TPU part last.\\n\\n| # | Part | File | Qty | Material |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 01 | Body / chassis | `01_body.stl` | 1 | PLA |\\n| 02 | Track cover, left | `02_track_cover_left.stl` | 1 | PLA |\\n| 03 | Track cover, right | `03_track_cover_right.stl` | 1 | PLA |\\n| 04 | Wheel | `04_wheel.stl` | 4 | PLA |\\n| 05 | Head base | `05_head_base.stl` | 1 | PLA |\\n| 06 | Head cover | `06_head_cover.stl` | 1 | PLA |\\n| 07 | Neck | `07_neck.stl` | 1 | PLA |\\n| 08 | Neck mount | `08_neck_mount.stl` | 1 | PLA |\\n| 09 | Track link | `09_track.stl` | many | TPU |\\n\\nThe eight PLA parts are 01–08; the track link is the only part in TPU, so it\\nsits last.\\n\\nThe numeric prefix is the only thing tying a mesh to its place in the assembly,\\nso keep the sequence contiguous and unique. If you add a part, renumber rather\\nthan reusing or skipping a number, and update this table in the same commit.\\n\\nWheel and track-link quantities are not yet confirmed against the assembly, and\\nthere is no printed-parts BOM with fasteners, bearings, and motor\\nspecifications yet. Both are open items.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Print settings\\n\\nNot yet characterised — these are starting points, not validated settings.\\n\\n| | |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Material | PLA for structure; TPU for the track links |\\n| Layer height | 0.08 mm |\\n| Walls | 3 |\\n| Infill | 20 % (structural parts), 40 % (neck and head mount) |\\n| Supports | Tree on the body (01) and neck (07); normal on the wheels (04) |\\n\\nThe track links are the one part that genuinely needs a flexible filament —\\nprinting them rigid gives you a robot that cannot corner. If you print a set,\\nplease open an issue with your material, printer, and what worked.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Whole-robot models\\n\\nTwo files carry the complete assembly — chassis, head, tracks, motors, servos,\\nelectronics — rather than a single printable part:\\n\\n| File | Size | For |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| `src/marvin_design_v3_hq.3dm` | 74 MB | Rhino, native |\\n| `step/marvin_robot.step` | 28 MB | any CAD that reads STEP AP242 |\\n\\nBoth come from the 930 MB Rhino working model. Most of that bulk was not real\\nNURBS: the heavy objects are degree-(1,1) 2x2 patches, i.e. flat triangles\\nwearing a NURBS costume, inherited from the cinematic mesh the design started\\nfrom. Converted to actual meshes they are ~100x cheaper and geometrically\\nidentical — `02_track_cover_left` alone is 147 MB as a Brep and 1.3 MB as a\\nmesh. Genuinely modelled parts (`07_neck`, `08_neck_mount`, bearings, battery,\\nand 814 smaller objects) are still NURBS and still editable.\\n\\nAccuracy against the source is 0.0000 mm on the converted parts and never worse\\nthan 0.05 mm anywhere in the STEP.\\n\\n**A caveat on the STEP.** It uses AP242 *tessellated* geometry, which is what\\nmakes 700k triangles fit in 28 MB — as a B-rep the same mesh would be about\\n700 MB, because a STEP face costs ~1 kB whether it holds a NURBS patch or a flat\\ntriangle. Verified to open in OpenCASCADE and FreeCAD. **Rhino 8 does not read\\ntessellated STEP** — Rhino users should take the `.3dm`, which is the same\\ngeometry.\\n\\n## Large files\\n\\nOne file exceeds what GitHub will accept and is **not in this repository**:\\n\\n| File | Size | Why it is excluded |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| `src/marvin_design.3dm` | 2.7 GB | Over GitHub's 100 MB file limit, and over Git LFS's 2 GB per-file cap |\\n\\nIt is listed in [`.gitignore`](../../.gitignore). To get it, ask for a copy — or,\\nbetter, help fix the underlying problem.\\n\\n**The problem was tessellation, not complexity.** `01_body.stl` used to be a\\n454 MB export — roughly nine million triangles for a part that is essentially a\\nprinted box — and had to be distributed out of band. Re-exported from Rhino at\\na coarser mesh tolerance it is 240 k triangles and 12 MB, with no visible\\ndetail lost at print resolution, so it now lives in the repository like every\\nother part. The whole `stl/` set is 22 MB.\\n\\nEvery mesh in `stl/`, the `.3dm` models and the `.step` export are tracked\\nthro…236 tokens truncated…ADME.md) protect the neck from hitting its\\n  mechanical stops. If the head geometry changes, update and physically verify\\n  those limits in the same pull request.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Licence\\n\\nEverything in this folder is licensed under the\\n[CERN Open Hardware Licence v2, Strongly Reciprocal](../../LICENSE-hardware) —\\nnot the MIT licence that covers the project's software.\\nhardware/mechanical/src/marvin_design_v3_hq.3dm\\nhardware/mechanical/README.md\\nhardware/mechanical/stl/09_track.stl\\nhardware/mechanical/stl/06_head_cover.stl\\nhardware/mechanical/stl/04_wheel.stl\\nhardware/mechanical/stl/03_track_cover_right.stl\\nhardware/mechanical/stl/08_neck_mount.stl\\nhardware/mechanical/stl/02_track_cover_left.stl\\nhardware/mechanical/stl/01_body.stl\\nhardware/mechanical/stl/05_head_base.stl\\nhardware/mechanical/stl/07_neck.stl\\nhardware/mechanical/step/marvin_robot.step\\nhardware/mechanical/3d_print/marvin_BambuLab_x1C.3mf\\nhardware/mechanical/3d_print/marvin_BambuLab_A1_Mini.3mf\\nhardware/mechanical/3d_print/marvin_generic_printer.3mf\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Git LFS — large binary design assets\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# CAD and mesh files are binary and get re-exported often. Storing them in LFS\\n# keeps them out of the packfile history, so a clone stays fast even after\\n# dozens of design revisions.\\n#\\n# Contributors need Git LFS installed *before* cloning:\\n#     git lfs install\\n# If you cloned first, run `git lfs pull` to fetch the real files.\\n\\n# Meshes\\n*.stl   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n*.3mf   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n*.obj   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n*.glb   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n\\n# CAD sources and interchange formats\\n*.3dm   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n*.step  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n*.stp   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n*.f3d   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n*.iges  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n\\n# Media and datasheets\\n*.pdf   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n*.png   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n*.jpg   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n*.mp4   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# The published website is the exception to all of the above\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# GitHub Pages serves files straight out of the repository and does NOT resolve\\n# Git LFS pointers. An LFS-tracked image or video reaches the browser as a\\n# ~130-byte text file, so the page silently breaks. These undo the media rules\\n# above for the published site only.\\n#\\n# Scope this to the patterns that actually match an LFS rule. A blanket\\n# `docs/** -filter -diff -merge` also unsets `diff`, which makes Git treat the\\n# site's HTML, CSS and JS as binary and stop showing their changes.\\napps/build-docs/**/*.png  -filter -diff -merge -text\\napps/build-docs/**/*.jpg  -filter -diff -merge -text\\napps/build-docs/**/*.webp -filter -diff -merge -text\\napps/build-docs/**/*.mp4  -filter -diff -merge -text\\napps/build-docs/**/*.mov  -filter -diff -merge -text\\napps/build-docs/**/*.pdf  -filter -diff -merge -text\\n\\n# Application and test screenshots ship directly from the repository too.\\napps/web/**/*.png          -filter -diff -merge -text\\napps/web/**/*.jpg          -filter -diff -merge -text\\napps/web/**/*.mp4          -filter -diff -merge -text\\ntests/acceptance/**/*.png  -filter -diff -merge -text\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Line endings\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n* text=auto eol=lf\\n*.sh    text eol=lf\\n*.bat   text eol=crlf\\n\\n# KiCad files are text and should diff as text\\n*.kicad_sch  text\\n*.kicad_pcb  text\\n*.kicad_pro  text\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# GitHub language statistics\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nhardware/mechanical/* linguist-documentation\\napps/build-docs/*     linguist-documentation\\n\\n# Offline human art source and generated crowd geometry.\\nscripts/city/*.blend filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\napps/simulator-macos/Resources/City/crowd.json filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\napps/simulator-macos/Resources/City/*.hdr filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a101fa-8585-7632-b697-28e4fd61ef3f","create_time":1791034883.618217}}}
